3	//! The generic proxy pipeline for an OpenAI-shaped route (SMA-769 D6, spec § 8.1).
4	//!
5	//! One [`Endpoint`] descriptor per route holds every step that is specific to the route: the
6	//! request parse, the non-stream usage reader, the stream usage reader, the terminal SSE frame
7	//! and the interception limits. [`proxy`] is the flow that is common to all of them. It is the
8	//! `/v1/chat/completions` handler flow, moved here step for step.
9	//!
10	//! By the time [`proxy`] runs, the [`require_iam_auth`](super::auth::require_iam_auth)
11	//! middleware has authenticated and authorized the caller and attached a [`CallerContext`].
12	//! The security rules of the chat handler apply unchanged to every route:
13	//!
14	//! - **Egress hygiene (load-bearing):** only the raw body bytes go to the OpenAI client. The
15	//!   caller's inbound headers never do. The caller's `Authorization: Bearer <paigasus-key>` must
16	//!   never reach OpenAI. The client builds fresh upstream headers from its own real key.
17	//! - The upstream response is forwarded verbatim: a full body (non-stream) or an UNBUFFERED SSE
18	//!   stream. A non-2xx upstream (OpenAI's own error envelope) passes through unchanged.
19	//! - The mid-stream terminal error and the cancel-on-drop contract are described on
20	//!   [`terminal_sse_error_stream`](super::relay::terminal_sse_error_stream).
21	
